● Now I have a complete picture. Here is the full plan.

  ---
  Plan: clear the medium/stripped XFAIL by restoring the irreducible-loop back-edge

  TL;DR

  The remaining gap is not an irreducible-loop discovery issue (the structurer already detects it, emits a label +
   goto) — it's a HIR post-cleanup pass that incorrectly deletes the goto. The fix is ~15 lines in
  label_cleanup.rs plus tests. Estimated to clear medium and stripped from XFAIL to PASS.

  Root cause (verified by tracing)

  The full pipeline for medium.elf's nth_prime outer loop is:

  1. Lifter / SSA: lifts the back-edge BB_64 → BB_31 correctly. ✓
  2. detect_irreducible_regions: identifies the multi-entry SCC. ✓
  3. split_irreducible_entries: greedy node-splitter tries every candidate, finds every split produces a larger
  irreducible body, and bails (see trace at /tmp/node-split.log lines 6-8: entry=BlockId(31) current_body=5 
  remaining_largest_body=29). Skipping is correct given its current heuristic. ✓ (but worth a follow-up — see §5)
  4. Structurer (structure_until at crates/unlace-struct/src/structurer.rs:2491): pre-allocates goto_targets for
  multi-pred non-loop-header blocks, allocates LabelId(4) for BB_64, walks BB_27.true → emits Label(4) + BB_64
  body + inner while loop + BB_32 if-then-else with then: [Goto(LabelId(4))]. ✓ Verified at
  /tmp/struct-trace4.log:271,319.
  5. label_cleanup::remove_same_scope_backedges (crates/unlace-struct/src/label_cleanup.rs:51-123): incorrectly 
  deletes the Goto(LabelId(4)) because it propagates the visible label set into nested If/While bodies.

  The HIR shape after the structurer (simplified) looks like:

  If(cond_27,
    then: [
      Label(4),                            // ← visible set adds LabelId(4) here
      ...BB_64 body...,
      While { ...inner is_prime... },
      If(cond_32,
        then: [Goto(4)],                   // ← still inside the same .then list
        else: [...]),
    ],
    else: [...])

  The cleanup walks then linearly, hits Label(4), adds it to visible, then descends into the inner
  If(cond_32).then with the inherited visible set, sees Goto(4), and deletes it as a "same-scope backedge". But
  the outer If(cond_32).then is a deeper lexical scope — the goto is not dead, it's the irreducible outer-loop
  back-edge.

  Result: nth_prime(10) executes one inner is_prime check, increments candidate once, falls through. Returns 3
  instead of 29. Skew of −26 propagates through hash_mix and the rest of main, giving final exit code 174 vs
  expected 33 (delta = 141 mod 256 = (-26 × 5 + offsets that get folded into the final mod-256)).

  The fix (Phase 1 — minimal change, expected to clear the XFAIL)

  File: crates/unlace-struct/src/label_cleanup.rs

  Change: remove_backedges_visible_from_scope should only delete a Goto(L) when L is visible in the same flat 
  scope, not when it's inherited from an outer scope. Concretely, when recursing into a nested scope
  (If/While/DoWhile/For/Switch/TryCatch/TryFinally bodies), pass an empty visible set rather than the inherited
  one — OR carry an additional "depth-of-definition" map and only remove when depth_of(label) == current_depth.

  Concrete patch (sketch):

  fn remove_same_scope_backedges(nodes: &mut Vec<HirNode>) {
      remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(nodes);
  }

  fn remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(nodes: &mut Vec<HirNode>) {
      let mut visible: BTreeSet<LabelId> = BTreeSet::new();
      let mut retained = Vec::with_capacity(nodes.len());

      for mut node in std::mem::take(nodes) {
          match &mut node {
              HirNode::Label(label) => {
                  visible.insert(*label);
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::Goto(label) if visible.contains(label) => {
                  if label_cleanup_trace_enabled() {
                      eprintln!("[unlace-label-cleanup] remove-same-scope-backedge label={label:?}");
                  }
                  // drop the redundant in-same-scope backward goto
              }
              HirNode::Block(children) => {
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(children); // ← no `&visible` inherited
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::If { then_body, else_body, .. } => {
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(then_body);
                  if let Some(else_body) = else_body {
                      remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(else_body);
                  }
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::While { body, .. }
              | HirNode::DoWhile { body, .. }
              | HirNode::For { body, .. } => {
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(body);
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::Switch { cases, default, .. } => {
                  for (_, body, _) in cases {
                      remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(body);
                  }
                  if let Some(default) = default {
                      remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(default);
                  }
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::TryCatch { try_body, catch_arms } => {
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(try_body);
                  for (_ty, body) in catch_arms.iter_mut() {
                      remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(body);
                  }
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              HirNode::TryFinally { try_body, finally_body } => {
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(try_body);
                  remove_backedges_in_flat_scope(finally_body);
                  retained.push(node);
              }
              _ => retained.push(node),
          }
      }
      *nodes = retained;
  }

  (The Block variant probably already breaks scope correctly; the bug is specifically that
  If/While/Switch/TryCatch/TryFinally were passing &visible instead of starting fresh.)

  Why this is the right semantic: a "redundant backward goto in the same scope" is only redundant when the goto
  and the label are siblings in the same Vec (no intervening conditional, no intervening loop). If the goto is one
   level deeper (inside an if's then-body), it's a conditional back-jump that re-executes the code between the
  label and the conditional — that's exactly an irreducible loop, never redundant.

