  What this fix does not do (deferred work, NOT required for XFAIL)

  These are real issues I noticed during investigation; calling them out so they don't get lost, but each is its
  own change:

  (a) split_irreducible_entries greedy heuristic is conservative

  crates/unlace-struct/src/node_split.rs:131-148 rejects a split if simulate_post_split_score.largest_body >= 
  region.body_blocks.len(). For multi-entry SCCs where individual splits expose larger SCCs (because the simulated
   CFG re-runs Tarjan on the whole function), every candidate looks "non-shrinking" and the pass bails — leaving
  the structurer to fall back to gotos.

  The gotos work fine if the cleanup doesn't eat them (Phase 1 fix). But ideally we'd convert these to natural
  loops. Possible follow-ups:
  - Tie-break on entry_count decrease even when largest_body doesn't strictly shrink.
  - Allow multi-round non-shrinking splits if total entry-count across all regions decreases.
  - Borrow Janssen-Corporaal's controlled node splitting (cited in node_split.rs doc-comment) more faithfully.

  Lower priority because Phase 1 already makes the irreducible case produce correct C; this would just make it
  prettier C.

  (b) condition_dup.rs may have similar inherited-scope bugs

  Worth grepping the structurer-adjacent passes (goto_elim.rs, condition_dup.rs, goto_classify.rs) for any other
  place that propagates a label/goto set across If/While bodies. Same-shape bug could exist elsewhere; would
  manifest as similar XFAILs on heavier-irreducible binaries.

  (c) find_join_point loop-header preference (already landed today)

  The peel-then-loop fix I landed in structurer.rs:2830 is independent of the goto cleanup fix. Both are needed:
  without my structurer fix, array_max/array_min are still broken; without the label_cleanup fix, nth_prime outer
  loop is still broken.

  (d) Tail-duplication tuning

  tail_duplicate_aggressive is gated behind a goto_elim parameter and not currently invoked in the main pipeline
  (only tail_duplicate_small_blocks runs). If Phase 1 still leaves gotos in some fixtures, switching to aggressive
   duplication might convert more cases into natural loops. Again — quality of output, not correctness.

  Risks & rollback

  - Low risk overall. The change is in a HIR post-cleanup pass that hasn't been touched in a way that affects
  correctness elsewhere.
  - Risk vector 1: If any test relied on the old buggy behavior — i.e., expected a cross-scope backward goto to be
   removed — that test will fail. I checked the existing removes_same_scope_backward_gotos test (line 475); it
  only tests flat-same-scope removal, so it's unaffected. Other tests in the module appear similarly scoped.
  - Risk vector 2: New surviving gotos could confuse other downstream passes. Specifically
  goto_elim::eliminate_forward_gotos runs after cleanup_unreachable_labels (per pipeline order). Forward-goto
  elimination shouldn't touch backward gotos. Should verify by tracing.
  - Rollback: trivially revertable single-file patch.

